// =============================================================================
// COCKPIT MEASURES - current-state operational view
// =============================================================================
//
// Add these to FactWorkOrder.tmdl using the same TMDL pattern as the existing
// 23 measures.
//
// Every expected value below was computed directly from the source CSVs. If a
// measure disagrees, the measure is wrong.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EXPECTED VALUES - whole dataset, no filters
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   Measure                       BEDFORD   HOUSTON    AURORA       ALL
//   Open Work Orders                  578     1,418       600     2,596
//   Overdue Work Orders               172       767       247     1,186
//   Overdue %                       29.8%     54.1%     41.2%     45.7%
//   Avg Days Overdue                 35.8      34.4      34.1      34.6
//   Oldest Open Work Order (Days)     113       119       115       119
//   Open Emergency Work Orders          5        25         4        34
//   PM Due Next 14 Days                74       108        74       256
//   Data As Of Date            2026-07-29 (all sites)
//
// =============================================================================
// THE ANCHOR - read this before using any measure below
// =============================================================================
// Every current-state measure needs an "as of" date. Three candidates, only one
// of which is correct:
//
//   TODAY()                        WRONG. The data ends 2026-07-29 and never
//                                  moves. TODAY() drifts further from it every
//                                  day, so overdue counts inflate over time and
//                                  a demo behaves differently each week.
//
//   MAX(FactWorkOrder[ReportDate]) WRONG. Returns 2026-09-09. Maximo generates
//                                  PM work orders LEADTIME days before they come
//                                  due, so 861 work orders are legitimately
//                                  dated after the data cutoff. Anchoring here
//                                  understates every overdue count.
//
//   MAX(DimDate[Date]) filtered    CORRECT. 2026-07-29, the true data cutoff.
//   to ISPASTORTODAY = 1
//
// REMOVEFILTERS(DimDate) appears on nearly every measure below for the same
// reason it appears on Backlog Hours: "open" and "overdue" are attributes of the
// row as it stands NOW, not of a time period. Without it, slicing by month
// silently changes the meaning to "work orders reported in that month that
// happen to still be open" - a different and much smaller number.
// =============================================================================


// Anchor for every current-state calculation. ISPASTORTODAY is set by the
// generator and marks every date at or before the data cutoff.
Data As Of Date =
CALCULATE (
    MAX ( DimDate[Date] ),
    DimDate[ISPASTORTODAY] = 1,
    REMOVEFILTERS ( DimDate )
)


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OVERDUE
// Expected: 1,186 all sites. Houston 767 of 1,418 open (54.1%) against
// Bedford's 172 of 578 (29.8%) - the sharpest single contrast in the dataset.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Overdue Work Orders =
VAR _AsOf = [Data As Of Date]
RETURN
    CALCULATE (
        COUNTROWS ( FactWorkOrder ),
        FactWorkOrder[IsOpen] = 1,
        FactWorkOrder[TargCompDate] < _AsOf,
        REMOVEFILTERS ( DimDate )
    )


// Expected: 45.7% all sites
Overdue % =
DIVIDE ( [Overdue Work Orders], [Open Work Orders] )


// AVERAGEX over the overdue rows, not an average of a column - the days-overdue
// value does not exist as a column and cannot, because it depends on the anchor.
// Expected: 34.6 days all sites
Avg Days Overdue =
VAR _AsOf = [Data As Of Date]
VAR _Overdue =
    CALCULATETABLE (
        FactWorkOrder,
        FactWorkOrder[IsOpen] = 1,
        FactWorkOrder[TargCompDate] < _AsOf,
        REMOVEFILTERS ( DimDate )
    )
RETURN
    AVERAGEX ( _Overdue, DATEDIFF ( FactWorkOrder[TargCompDate], _AsOf, DAY ) )


// Future-dated PM work orders produce a negative age here, which is correct -
// they have not been raised yet. MAXX ignores them.
// Expected: 119 days all sites
Oldest Open Work Order (Days) =
VAR _AsOf = [Data As Of Date]
VAR _Open =
    CALCULATETABLE (
        FactWorkOrder,
        FactWorkOrder[IsOpen] = 1,
        REMOVEFILTERS ( DimDate )
    )
RETURN
    MAXX ( _Open, DATEDIFF ( FactWorkOrder[ReportDate], _AsOf, DAY ) )


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TODAY'S PRIORITIES
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Emergency work still open is the single most actionable number on the page.
// Expected: 34 all sites - Houston 25, Bedford 5, Aurora 4.
Open Emergency Work Orders =
CALCULATE (
    COUNTROWS ( FactWorkOrder ),
    FactWorkOrder[IsOpen] = 1,
    FactWorkOrder[WORKTYPE] = "EM",
    REMOVEFILTERS ( DimDate )
)


// Forward PM load. Uses TargCompDate, not ReportDate, because what matters is
// when the work comes due - not when Maximo generated the record.
// Expected: 256 all sites
PM Due Next 14 Days =
VAR _AsOf = [Data As Of Date]
RETURN
    CALCULATE (
        COUNTROWS ( FactWorkOrder ),
        FactWorkOrder[IsOpen] = 1,
        FactWorkOrder[IsPM] = 1,
        FactWorkOrder[TargCompDate] >= _AsOf,
        FactWorkOrder[TargCompDate] <= _AsOf + 14,
        REMOVEFILTERS ( DimDate )
    )


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THROUGHPUT
// Completion is measured on ActFinishDate, which requires the inactive
// relationship. Without USERELATIONSHIP this silently reports completions
// against the month the work was REPORTED, which is not the same thing.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Work Orders Completed =
CALCULATE (
    COUNTROWS ( FactWorkOrder ),
    FactWorkOrder[IsCompleted] = 1,
    USERELATIONSHIP ( DimDate[Date], FactWorkOrder[ActFinishDate] )
)


Work Orders Reported =
CALCULATE (
    COUNTROWS ( FactWorkOrder ),
    FactWorkOrder[IsCancelled] = 0
)


// Positive means the backlog grew in the period. Pair the two on a monthly
// column chart - it is the clearest way to show a backlog going the wrong way.
Net Backlog Change =
[Work Orders Reported] - [Work Orders Completed]


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CAPACITY
// Backlog Hours / headcount, not / weekly hours. Answers "how many hours is
// each technician personally carrying", which crews understand immediately.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Backlog Hours per Technician =
DIVIDE (
    [Backlog Hours],
    DIVIDE ( [Weekly Craft Capacity (Hours)], 40 )
)


// =============================================================================
// WHAT THIS MODEL CANNOT DO - do not build visuals that need these
// =============================================================================
//
// BACKLOG BY CRAFT. There is no craft column on WORKORDER, and that is faithful
// to Maximo - craft lives on job plans and assignments, not the work order
// header. LABTRANS carries CRAFT but only for work already performed, so open
// work has none by definition. Use CREWID (9 crews) or WOPRIORITY instead;
// crew is the better operational cut anyway because it maps to how work is
// actually assigned.
//
// SLA OR RESPONSE-TIME COMPLIANCE. No contract dimension and no SLA target
// fields exist. Adding a contract dimension keyed to site or location is the
// natural extension - name it as a roadmap item rather than faking it.
//
// TECHNICIAN UTILISATION AGAINST AVAILABLE HOURS. Partially possible from
// LABTRANS hours against headcount x 40, but there is no attendance, leave, or
// non-productive time, so any "utilisation %" would be a ratio of two things
// that do not share a denominator. Show labour hours by craft instead.
//
// A COMPOSITE ASSET HEALTH SCORE. Technically buildable, deliberately omitted.
// A single number blending failures, downtime, age, and cost is unfalsifiable
// without published weights, and invites the one question with no good answer.
//
// =============================================================================
// KNOWN DATA ARTIFACT - affects any intake-vs-completion trend
// =============================================================================
// The generator deliberately leaves recent work open, ramping up for work under
// 120 days old, to produce a realistic current backlog. The side effect is that
// the final ~2 months show far more work reported than completed AT EVERY SITE,
// including healthy Bedford (317 reported vs 194 completed in the last 30 days).
//
// That tail is an artifact of construction, not a site-performance signal.
// Plot Work Orders Reported vs Work Orders Completed by MONTH across the full
// window, where the artifact is confined to the right edge - and say so if
// anyone asks. Do not build a "last 30 days" intake-vs-completion card.
